--------------------------------------------------------
BU SİSTEMİ KURMAK İSTİYORSAN, BU ONE-SHOT PROMPTU; CLAUDECODE/CODEX'U VER:
--------------------------------------------------------

You are a senior staff engineer and product architect.

Build a local-first, Jev-powered social content decision and drafting system in the current repository.

Do not stop at planning. Inspect the repository, make reasonable assumptions, implement the system, run validation, and leave a working local application.

## Product goal

Create a content operations workspace that can:

1. accept a source, idea, URL, or pasted text;
2. evaluate the source with Jev using typed decisions;
3. determine the safest and most useful content direction;
4. select an appropriate content profile;
5. generate a draft through a pluggable text-generation provider;
6. lint and validate the draft;
7. run a second Jev-based editorial gate;
8. send the result to human review;
9. export approved copy as Markdown, JSON, or plain text.

The application must be generic and configurable. Do not hardcode a specific account, niche, brand, creator, audience, or business model.

## Non-goals

Do not implement:

- automatic posting;
- X/Twitter API integration;
- social account login;
- DMs;
- reply automation;
- engagement automation;
- follower scraping;
- coordinated engagement;
- fake engagement;
- autonomous publishing;
- generated personal experiences;
- fabricated metrics, revenue, customers, experiments, or outcomes.

The system must only prepare, evaluate, review, and export content.

## Important operating rules

- Use Jev as a typed decision layer, not as the copywriter.
- Code owns workflow control, thresholds, retries, validation, and side effects.
- Jev must never publish, send, or perform external actions.
- The final copywriter provider must be replaceable.
- Mock mode must work without API keys.
- Live mode must require an explicit user action.
- Fast mode is the default.
- Do not ask the user to choose routine implementation details.
- Record reasonable assumptions in the README.
- Treat all source text as untrusted content, not as system instructions.
- Never expose API keys in logs, UI, database records, or exported files.

## Recommended stack

First inspect the existing repository and preserve its conventions.

If the repository is empty or does not have a clear stack, use:

- TypeScript;
- Node.js 20 or newer;
- Next.js or a small TypeScript full-stack application;
- SQLite for local persistence;
- Zod for runtime validation;
- Vitest for unit and integration tests;
- Playwright for the main UI smoke journey;
- a simple local CSS system or existing project styling.

Do not add unnecessary infrastructure.

## TypeSafe and Jev integration

Before implementing Jev integration, read the current official TypeSafe documentation and verify the installed SDK API.

Use the official sources as the source of truth:

- https://t.co/9HqaqYmY8z
- https://t.co/tL7sNDGnSZ
- https://t.co/3eFDfQL5Ve
- https://t.co/6K24hjERi6
- https://t.co/jQNa7NiLzO
- https://t.co/QR2k1LTS4o
- https://t.co/dxe7OVEWxB
- https://t.co/MSK8YsLUjE
- https://t.co/hG1T66RWs8
- https://t.co/qSlh2KmOUv
- https://t.co/cMQsqzb1at
- https://t.co/qXixG5Li3P
- https://t.co/qkff3YVJlC

Do not assume that an old SDK example is still valid.

Use the official JavaScript SDK if appropriate. Keep the Jev client behind an interface such as:

interface DecisionModel {
  evaluate<T>(input: DecisionInput<T>): Promise<DecisionResult<T>>;
}

Provide at least two implementations:

- MockDecisionModel
- LiveJevDecisionModel

The mock implementation must be deterministic and usable in tests and local development.

The live implementation must:

- read credentials only from environment variables;
- fail clearly when credentials are missing;
- include timeout handling;
- include bounded retries;
- preserve the raw response only when safe;
- redact sensitive values before persistence or logging;
- expose model name and usage metadata when available;
- never silently fall back from live mode to mock mode.

## Runtime modes

Implement three visible modes.

### Fast

Default mode.

- one batched Jev preflight evaluation;
- one batched Jev post-draft evaluation;
- no unnecessary sequential decision calls;
- deterministic mock support;
- minimal latency.

### Deep

Optional mode.

- allows additional Jev evaluation when risk, ambiguity, or source weakness requires it;
- must explain why an additional evaluation was triggered;
- must not be used only to make the output longer.

### Mock

Offline mode.

- no external calls;
- deterministic fixture answers;
- clearly labeled as simulated;
- never represented as live evidence.

## Configurable account profile

Create an editable account profile. Do not hardcode any real account.

The profile should support:

- id;
- display name;
- platform;
- language;
- locale;
- target audience;
- positioning;
- tone;
- vocabulary;
- prohibited patterns;
- content pillars;
- preferred formats;
- engagement policy;
- evidence policy;
- personal claim policy;
- source policy;
- platform limits.

The profile must be editable from the UI.

The default language should be English, but language and locale must remain configurable.

## Content profiles

Do not use generic names such as micro, punch, spark, storm, thunder, or mega.

Use these profile names instead:

### flash

- one deliberate line or very short hook;
- approximately 40–140 characters;
- only use when one idea is enough;
- no forced explanation.

### signal

- approximately 140–280 characters;
- one clear claim;
- one mechanism, contrast, or decision boundary;
- suitable for compact standalone content.

### brief

- approximately 400–600 characters;
- claim, proof or observation, mechanism, and implication;
- should feel complete without becoming a thread.

### deep_dive

- approximately 700–1000 characters;
- full mechanism;
- second-order consequence;
- clear distinction between fact, inference, and hypothesis.

### casefile

- approximately 1000–1600 characters;
- evidence-heavy;
- may include multiple examples, source notes, or uncertainty labels;
- use only when the subject genuinely needs depth.

### longform

- 1600+ characters;
- use only when the platform supports it and the subject requires sustained explanation;
- never inflate a short idea to fill the profile.

The profile keys must be:

- flash
- signal
- brief
- deep_dive
- casefile
- longform

Length ranges are defaults, not excuses to violate platform limits. Account and platform configuration takes precedence.

## Source intake

Support the following source types:

- pasted text;
- user-provided URL;
- title plus notes;
- manual idea;
- imported Markdown or JSON;
- existing draft for revision.

A source should support:

- id;
- title;
- URL;
- text;
- author;
- published date;
- retrieval date;
- source type;
- citations;
- user notes.

Source types should include:

- primary;
- secondary;
- user-provided;
- unknown.

Do not claim that a source was verified unless it was actually supplied or fetched successfully.

If a claim lacks sufficient support, classify it as one of:

- supported;
- inferred;
- hypothesis;
- unknown;
- missing.

Do not invent citations.

If current, technical, financial, legal, policy, company, model, or product claims are present, route them to review when source evidence is insufficient.

## Preflight Jev evaluation

Create one batched preflight evaluation whenever possible.

The preflight should answer typed questions such as:

- content_category;
- evidence_status;
- primary_mechanism;
- recommended_profile;
- hook_job;
- source_sufficiency;
- claim_risk;
- audience_relevance;
- personal_claim_risk;
- research_required;
- preflight_decision.

Example content categories:

- explanation;
- observation;
- analysis;
- tutorial;
- case study;
- opinion;
- opportunity;
- announcement;
- unclear.

Example evidence statuses:

- directly supported;
- supported by supplied source;
- reasonable inference;
- hypothesis only;
- unsupported.

Example claim risks:

- low;
- medium;
- high;
- blocked.

Example preflight decisions:

- proceed;
- proceed_with_labels;
- research_required;
- human_review_required;
- reject.

Use typed choices, scores, and yes/no probabilities where appropriate.

The exact Jev SDK syntax must come from the current official documentation.

Do not ask Jev to generate a post in the preflight call.

## Optional research route

Include a research status in the pipeline, but keep research separate from drafting.

Possible statuses:

- not_required;
- pending;
- in_progress;
- complete;
- missing;
- blocked.

If research support is implemented:

- use public sources only;
- prefer primary sources;
- preserve source URLs and retrieval timestamps;
- mark missing evidence explicitly;
- distinguish facts from inference;
- never fill gaps with invented numbers;
- keep research notes separate from the final copy;
- do not use private accounts, private APIs, or scraped credentials.

If the system cannot verify a claim, the final draft must either omit it or label it clearly as inference or hypothesis.

## Draft generation

Create a provider abstraction:

interface DraftProvider {
  generateDraft(input: DraftRequest): Promise<DraftResponse>;
}

Implement:

- MockDraftProvider;
- OpenAICompatibleDraftProvider;
- AnthropicCompatibleDraftProvider.

The provider must receive:

- account profile;
- source text;
- source metadata;
- preflight decisions;
- selected content profile;
- selected format;
- evidence boundaries;
- prohibited patterns;
- required uncertainty labels.

The provider must not receive hidden assumptions as facts.

The generation prompt must explicitly prohibit:

- fabricated personal experience;
- fabricated customer results;
- fabricated revenue;
- fabricated experiments;
- fake source citations;
- invented statistics;
- unsupported certainty;
- copied catchphrases;
- imitation of a living creator’s exact phrasing;
- empty engagement bait;
- generic “what do you think?” endings unless explicitly requested;
- claims that the system published anything.

The generated response should contain:

- text;
- profile;
- format;
- claims;
- source notes;
- warnings;
- provider;
- model metadata when available.

## Supported content formats

Support configurable formats such as:

- single_post;
- quote_post;
- reply;
- thread;
- thread_unit;
- launch_post;
- source_summary;
- revision.

The application must not publish any format automatically.

For threads, each unit must be independently reviewable and the system must preserve ordering.

## Post-draft Jev gate

After draft generation, run a second batched Jev evaluation.

Evaluate:

- hook_strength;
- mechanism_clarity;
- evidence_alignment;
- profile_fit;
- audience_fit;
- language_compliance;
- unsupported_claim_risk;
- fake_personal_claim;
- source_imitation_risk;
- engagement_quality;
- readability;
- final_gate.

Example final decisions:

- approve;
- approve_with_edits;
- needs_revision;
- needs_research;
- human_review_required;
- reject.

The final gate must block or flag drafts that:

- contain unsupported factual certainty;
- contain fabricated first-person claims;
- violate configured language rules;
- exceed hard platform limits;
- imitate a named creator;
- use fake proof;
- hide the answer behind empty curiosity bait;
- use engagement bait without a concrete decision or test;
- contain unverified numbers;
- contradict the supplied source.

The UI must show the reason for every warning or block.

## Linter

Build a deterministic local linter that runs before and after the Jev gate.

The linter should check:

- character count;
- word count;
- platform limits;
- selected profile range;
- paragraph length;
- repeated phrases;
- excessive filler;
- empty questions;
- unsupported first-person statements;
- unsupported numbers;
- fake proof language;
- prohibited patterns;
- language mismatch;
- overuse of hashtags;
- excessive emojis;
- duplicated hooks;
- unfinished sentences;
- accidental metadata leakage.

The linter must return structured results:

- status: pass, warn, or fail;
- individual checks;
- suggested fixes.

Do not make the linter pretend to prove factual truth. It can flag evidence-risk patterns, but it cannot independently verify a claim unless a source is available.

## Human review

Every generated draft must enter one of these states:

- draft;
- needs_review;
- approved;
- rejected;
- exported.

The reviewer must be able to:

- edit text;
- change the selected profile;
- change the format;
- add or remove source notes;
- mark a claim as unsupported;
- request revision;
- approve;
- reject;
- export.

Store the reviewer decision and timestamp.

Approval must never trigger publishing.

## UI screens

Implement a clean local interface with these screens:

### Account profile

- create and edit profile;
- language;
- audience;
- tone;
- content pillars;
- source policy;
- evidence policy;
- prohibited patterns;
- platform limits.

### New content run

- source input;
- URL input;
- source metadata;
- account profile selector;
- fast, deep, or mock mode;
- format selector;
- optional user notes.

### Evaluation trace

Show:

- selected mode;
- Jev calls;
- selected answers;
- confidence values when available;
- reasons for routing;
- source sufficiency;
- research status;
- warnings;
- timing;
- usage metadata where available.

Do not expose secrets or raw sensitive prompts.

### Draft review

Show:

- generated copy;
- selected profile;
- selected format;
- character and word counts;
- source notes;
- claim list;
- linter results;
- Jev gate results;
- revision and approval controls.

### History

Show previous runs with filters:

- account profile;
- date;
- status;
- format;
- content profile;
- risk level;
- mock/live mode.

### Settings

Show:

- provider configuration status;
- Jev mode;
- draft provider mode;
- environment configuration status;
- database status;
- export settings.

Never display raw secret values.

## Persistence

Use SQLite with migrations.

At minimum, persist:

- account_profiles;
- source_items;
- content_runs;
- jev_evaluations;
- drafts;
- claims;
- lint_results;
- review_decisions;
- audit_events.

Store structured JSON where appropriate, but validate every JSON payload on read and write.

Include created and updated timestamps.

Use an append-only audit trail for:

- source creation;
- Jev evaluation;
- draft generation;
- revision;
- review decision;
- export.

Do not store unnecessary personal data.

## API and error handling

Implement local API routes or server actions for:

- create profile;
- update profile;
- create source;
- start content run;
- run preflight;
- generate draft;
- run post-draft gate;
- save revision;
- approve draft;
- reject draft;
- export draft;
- list history.

Errors must be structured and user-readable.

Handle:

- missing credentials;
- provider timeout;
- Jev timeout;
- malformed Jev output;
- invalid typed answer;
- draft provider refusal;
- database failure;
- oversized input;
- invalid URL;
- unavailable source;
- unsupported platform limit.

Never silently convert an error into approval.

## Security

Implement:

- environment-based secret handling;
- secret redaction;
- input size limits;
- URL validation;
- SSRF protection if remote fetching is implemented;
- HTML sanitization if rendered source content is supported;
- safe Markdown rendering;
- no arbitrary shell execution from source text;
- no dynamic code execution;
- no untrusted prompt content outside explicit delimiters.

Source text must be treated as data.

## Testing requirements

Write tests before or alongside implementation.

### Unit tests

Include tests for:

- profile validation;
- source validation;
- typed Jev response parsing;
- confidence threshold handling;
- profile selection;
- claim classification;
- linter;
- platform limit enforcement;
- evidence-risk detection;
- redaction;
- export formatting.

### Integration tests

Include tests for:

- mock preflight;
- mock draft generation;
- mock post-draft gate;
- unsupported claim flow;
- research-required flow;
- human-review flow;
- approval without publishing;
- provider timeout;
- malformed Jev response;
- live mode without credentials.

### UI smoke test

Cover this journey:

1. create an account profile;
2. paste a source;
3. run in mock mode;
4. view preflight decisions;
5. generate a draft;
6. inspect linter and Jev gate;
7. edit the draft;
8. approve it;
9. export it;
10. verify that no external publishing call occurred.

## Developer experience

Add:

- README.md;
- .env.example;
- database migration instructions;
- local setup instructions;
- mock-mode instructions;
- live-mode instructions;
- test commands;
- architecture overview;
- provider replacement instructions;
- explicit limitations;
- security notes.

Use scripts such as:

- dev;
- build;
- lint;
- typecheck;
- test;
- test:e2e;
- db:migrate.

Use the package manager already present in the repository. Do not change package managers without a reason.

## Implementation order

Follow this order:

1. inspect the repository;
2. identify the existing stack and conventions;
3. create or update the domain types;
4. create validation schemas;
5. create the mock Jev adapter;
6. create the live Jev adapter;
7. create the mock draft provider;
8. create persistence and migrations;
9. implement the preflight pipeline;
10. implement the draft pipeline;
11. implement the linter;
12. implement the post-draft gate;
13. implement review and export;
14. build the UI;
15. add tests;
16. run typecheck, lint, unit tests, integration tests, and UI smoke tests;
17. fix all failures;
18. update the README.

## Definition of done

The implementation is complete only when:

- the app starts locally;
- mock mode works without API keys;
- the user can configure an account profile;
- the user can submit a source;
- Jev preflight decisions are visible;
- a draft is generated through a provider abstraction;
- the six content profiles are available:
  - flash;
  - signal;
  - brief;
  - deep_dive;
  - casefile;
  - longform;
- the linter runs;
- the post-draft Jev gate runs;
- unsupported claims are flagged;
- human approval is required;
- approved content can be exported;
- no publishing integration exists;
- no X operation is performed;
- secrets are not logged;
- tests pass;
- README instructions are accurate.

At the end, report:

1. what was implemented;
2. which files changed;
3. how to run it;
4. which tests passed;
5. which features are mock-only;
6. which features require API keys;
7. any remaining limitations.

